    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_c142cd2d426c5f3da6af44ed27f491c24a6e81588191e213afe5445264a29ddd",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: identity (8) rewrites π(x)−li(x) as an endpoint term in θ(x)−x plus a weighted integral of θ(t)−t and fixed lower-end terms. Interpretation: this gives a one-way transfer of a pointwise θ-error bound to the π scale; it does not preserve or invert all information in the θ error. The large range is discharged by an antiderivative majorant g, while the low range is reduced to prime intervals: because π is constant and li increases on [p_k,p_{k+1}), li(x)−π(x) is largest at the next-prime endpoint (as an endpoint bound), leaving finitely many reported checks. The gain is a clean analytic/discrete splice; the remaining burden is the formula-sensitive majorization and the unpreserved computations.",
